# Comments

## When to write one

Write a comment only when the code cannot express the reason itself:

- A non-obvious constraint the code must satisfy (API limit, ordering requirement, browser/OS quirk).
- A deliberate tradeoff (why this approach was chosen over the obvious one).
- A workaround for an external bug or behavior you cannot fix here.
- A public-facing contract that isn't enforced by types or tests.

## When not to write one

- Do not restate what the code does. `// increment counter` above `i++` is noise.
- Do not narrate changes (`// added null check`) — that belongs in the commit message.
- Do not add conversational filler (`// just in case`, `// hopefully works`).
- Do not leave commented-out code. Delete it; version control remembers.
- Do not document obvious language semantics (`// returns a promise`) — trust the reader.

## Format

- One short, factual line. If it needs a paragraph, the code or the design is the problem.
- Place the comment directly above the code it explains, at the same indentation.
- Use `TODO(name): reason` and `FIXME(name): reason` for deferred work — the name makes ownership visible.
- Prefer naming and structure over comments. A well-named function removes the need for half the comments you'd otherwise write.

## Preserving existing comments

- Never delete a comment that explains *why* something is done, even if it looks redundant to you.
- If a comment is stale (code changed, reason no longer holds), update it — do not silently remove it.
- If a `TODO`/`FIXME` is resolved, delete it along with the code it described.
- When refactoring, carry comments forward to the equivalent code in the new structure.

## Doc comments

- Public APIs get a doc comment: what it does, what it returns, what it throws, and any non-obvious precondition.
- Internal helpers get a doc comment only if the signature doesn't make the contract obvious.
- Do not duplicate type information that the compiler already enforces.
